  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Data Collection for Evidence-Based Response and Provision of CCCM Services to Address the Needs of Populations Displaced by the Tonga Crisis</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the escalation of violence in Panyikang, Fashoda, Fangak, and Canal Pigi, over 15,000 internally displaced persons (IDPs) arrived in Malakal seeking refuge within the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS) Protection of Civilian (PoC) Site. To alleviate the suffering of IDPs within the site, the Humanitarian Country Team decided to relocate education and recreational services outside the PoC into an adjacent area. As the leading Camp Coordination and Camp Management (CCCM) actor on the ground, IOM is supporting communities and partners with necessary actions within the PoC and adjacent Sector 5, including grading and compacting of the open spaces in the buffer zone, demarcation of the shelter plots, site planning, and other heavy infrastructure work. Additionally, to facilitate the response within the PoC and surrounding counties, IOM&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) is scaling up data collection to provide partners with up-to-date, actionable, and verified information on population movement.</narrative>
  </description>
